You can replace this:

 for (k in 1:K){
      xi_r[k]~normal(0,1);

with just

  to_vector(xi_r) ~ normal(0, 1);

for a bit more savings.  It requires making xi_r
a matrix.

Same with this:

>  for (p in 1:P){
>       taup[p,1] ~ inv_gamma(.1,.1);
>       taup[p,2] ~ inv_gamma(.1,.1);
>    }

which can just be

  to_vector(taup) ~ inv_gamma(0.1, 0.1);

But that would require defining taup as
and xi_r as matrices.

BUT, we don't recommend these inverse gamma priors.

You can also replace these

>  mup[1] ~ normal(mupm[1], taup[1]);
>    mup[2] ~ normal(mupm[2], taup[2]);

with

  mup ~ normal(mupm, taup);

You can also vectorize this:

>  for (n in 1:N){
>       score[n] ~ bernoulli_logit(xi[item[n],1] * (theta[peeps[n]] - xi[item[n],2]));
>    }

score ~ bernoulli_logit(xi[item, 1] .* (theta[peeps] - xi[item, 2]));

