Hi again Andrew,

I have been scratching my head trying to think how the steps you are suggesting are different from what I have done already to evaluate the model. Sorry, I'm probably showing my ignorance here. If your suggestion is different from what I've been doing using the simulation code pasted below, and there is a chapter or place in BDA or G&H I have forgotten where I can read up, that'd be great.

In any case, after a little more tinkering and thinking about your comment, I did notice something. If I sim the betas using this code with rnorm(0, 5), which was my prior, it really blows up the model in the ways I mentioned above, but to the extreme. With the original rnorm(0, 1), it was just quirky, but rnorm(0, 5) is a mess. So, I tried with betas drawn from rnorm(0, 0.1) and everything converged, including lp__, and coefficient plots matched up like one would expect. So, it appears that high variance or large betas maybe causes some problems with "identifiability" of Omega. Not sure where that gets me, but there it was.

set.seed(1234)

K <- 7;
D <- 4;
N <- 100;

# stick breaking construction to generate a random correlation matrix
L_Omega <- matrix(0,D,D);
L_Omega[1,1] <- 1;
for (i in 2:D) {
  bound <- 1;
  for (j in 1:(i-1)) {
    L_Omega[i,j] <- runif(1, -sqrt(bound), sqrt(bound));
    bound <- bound - L_Omega[i,j]^2;
  }
  L_Omega[i,i] <- sqrt(bound);
}

Omega <- L_Omega %*% t(L_Omega);

x <- matrix(rnorm(N * K, 0, 1), N, K);

beta <- matrix(rnorm(D * K, 0, 5), D, K);

z <- matrix(NA, N, D);

for (n in 1:N) {

  z[n,] <- mvrnorm(1, x[n,] %*% t(beta), Omega);
}

y <- matrix(0, N, D);

for (n in 1:N) {

  for (d in 1:D) {
    y[n,d] <- ifelse((z[n,d] > 0), 1, 0);
  }
}

