Thanks for your message. I think you are right - essentially  'rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset' is of type real because you have:
real<lower=0,upper=1> * real<lower=0> + real<lower=0>, which gets promoted to a real, rather than real<lower=0>, as you might expect.

Am not sure this'll work - but it is worth trying. Define rightprob as a real, rather than real<lower=0,upper=1>; since you're passing the data, you will need to check that it satisfies your constraints before the data goes into Stan. Based on my own models (which have heteroscedasticity) I think this may work. Let me know.

Alternatively, you could try defining a transformed parameter:

transformed parameter{
    real<lower=0> aSigma;
    aSigma <- rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset;
}

And using aSigma instead for your normal standard deviation? You may suffer the same issue again: Stan may not like trying to coerce the RHS to a type real<lower=0>.

If neither of these work, my apologies! (If you attach a R data file with all the necessary data variables, then this may make debugging quicker.)

Best,

Ben

Sorry - it was a silly mistake on my part. The issue is the following: if you use an assignment operator '<-', you need to use normal_rng(). Are you trying to generate fake data, then sample from it? If so, then you want to generate fake data using normal_rng because you want Stan to generate (pseudo)-random samples from a normal.

If this is the case, may I suggest the following: you generate the data in one Stan file, using the 'generated quantities' block as below (obviously you need to pass it data, etc.) To do this use the algorithm='Fixed_param' option when you call stan in R; you won't have any parameters in your model so you need to tell Stan not to look for them.

generated quantities{
 real perceived_rprob[nTrials];
  ....

  for(trial_i in 1:nTrials){
       perceived_rprob[trial_i] <- normal_rng(rightprob[trial_i], rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);
        ...
   }

}

You then collect the samples for your various objects, and then fit your model using (in a separate Stan file):

model{
    for (trial_i in 1:NTrials){
        ....
        choices_01[trial_i] ~ bernoulli_logit(lambda*(Rval-Lval));
    }

}

There may be a way to fit it all in one Stan file, but think doing the above makes life easier.

Let me know if the above is what you want, and makes sense.

Best,

Ben

Hi Christine,

You won't get the error message if you use the '~' statement rather than '<-', I hope...

How many parameters/trials are we talking about? I often fit models with tens of thousands of parameters... You said earlier that you wanted to infer Perceived_rprob? Do you mean, it's more of a nuisance parameter? (Necessary for the resultant inferences, but not useful in itself.) If that is the case, then you are right to define it at the top of the model block, as opposed to in the parameters block. I do think that hierarchical parameters would work nicely here, yes.

I have just tried the following, and it runs. However, it looks like there are issues with the model still, but could be due to your generated data...(Are the probabilities supposed to be 1s and 0s mostly?) Also I think that since you are dealing with a variable constrained to lie between 0 and 1, you need to either:

- Take the logit of your rightprob variables etc. to make these unconstrained.
- Choose a different sampling model, for example, a beta distribution.

model {

    real Rval[nTrials];

    real Lval[nTrials];

    real perceived_rprob[nTrials];

    real perceived_lprob[nTrials];

    real perceived_rvol[nTrials];

    real perceived_lvol[nTrials];

    rho ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);

    for (trial_i in 1:nTrials) {

    rightprob[trial_i] ~ normal(perceived_rprob[trial_i],perceived_rprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

    leftprob[trial_i] ~ normal(perceived_lprob[trial_i], perceived_lprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

    rightvol[trial_i] ~ normal(perceived_rvol[trial_i], perceived_rvol[trial_i]*vol_m+vol_offset);

    leftvol[trial_i] ~ normal(perceived_lvol[trial_i], perceived_lvol[trial_i]*vol_m+vol_offset);

    Rval[trial_i] <- exp(-(-log(perceived_rprob[trial_i]))^alpha)*(perceived_rvol[trial_i]^rho);

    Lval[trial_i] <- exp(-(-log(perceived_lprob[trial_i]))^alpha)*(perceived_lvol[trial_i]^rho);

    choices_01[trial_i] ~ bernoulli_logit(lambda*(Rval[trial_i]-Lval[trial_i]));

    }

}

